/* book exam traps: Drawing the rotation the wrong way. Read the arrow on the device; reversing the magnet turns 1 → 2 → 3 into 1 → 3 → 2. | Treating an isolator as reciprocal. S₂₁ ≈ 1 and S₁₂ ≈ 0, so its loss is not the same both ways. | Calling the ideal circulator lossy because it is nonreciprocal. Its matrix is unitary: it is lossless and matched, but not symmetric. | Leaving a circulator port open. Its reflection circulates to the next port and isolation collapses. | Confusing isolation with return loss. Isolation is reverse transmission (−20 log₁₀|S₁₂|); return loss is reflection at a port (−20 log₁₀|S₁₁|), both quoted as positive dB. */
